      "script": "MYNA 1\nOld names always have a \"vibe,\" don't you think? Like Ang Mo Kio.\n\nMYNA 2\nYa lor. Red Hair Man Bridge. Ang Mo means \"Red Hair\" in Hokkien—how people used to\ndescribe the Caucasians.\n\nMYNA 1\nYou think it refers to Sir Thompson’s hair? He was quite the Ang Mo (Westerner).\n\nMYNA 2\nI still think it refers to tomatoes. Red and round! Much better than a bridge.\n\nRECLAIMED ARCHIVE\nSpelling is fixed. \nMeaning is still in motion.\n\nMYNA 1\nDoes it not remind us of the red hair of Sir Thumbel Thompson?\n\nMYNA 2\nI like to refer it to tomatoes.\n\nBUS 166\nApproaching Kebun Baru. \nA unique place where birds gather to sing. \nEvery Sunday, bird singing competitions take place here.\nYou may step off Ang Mo Kio Pri School.\nOr stay with me.\nPlease note that birds are not allowed on the bus!\n\nMYNA 1(Indignant)\nYou hear that? The bus says birds not allowed! Discrimination leh!\n\nMYNA 2\n\nDon't care lah. Does the 166 think it can see us through the CCTV? We are already here!\n\nMYNA 1\nWe won't drop anything, we are \"high-class\" birds. We only drop some birdsong for the people.\n\nMYNA 2\nListen! My friends are there! The Mata Puteh—the Oriental White-eye—and the Zebra Doves.\n\nMYNA 1\nThe names might stay on the signs, but the sounds... the sounds linger in the air long after we\nfly away.",
      "notes": [
        "Spoken text follows the recording; unspoken stage directions and explanatory translations are omitted.",
        "The recording omits the repeated Thompson/tomatoes exchange in the PDF."
      ],
